  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Since $\\sigma(x,y)=\\sigma(y,x)$, covariances are symmetrical. Furthermore, from the definition of the variance and covariance,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        132.0,
        161.0,
        1094.0,
        246.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula012]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        521.0,
        258.0,
        711.0,
        299.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "i.e., the covariance of a variable with itself is the variance of that variable. It also follows from Equation 3.8 that, for any constant a,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        138.0,
        313.0,
        1098.0,
        398.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula013]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        519.0,
        414.0,
        659.0,
        448.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula014]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        502.0,
        453.0,
        739.0,
        489.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "and if b is also a constant",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b11"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        145.0,
        519.0,
        449.0,
        554.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula015]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b12"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        488.0,
        571.0,
        759.0,
        608.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "From Equations 3.10a and 3.10d,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b14"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        148.0,
        634.0,
        541.0,
        674.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula016]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b15"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        516.0,
        686.0,
        735.0,
        726.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "i.e., the variance of the transformed variable $ a x $ is $ a^2 $ times the variance of $ x $. Likewise, for any constant $ a $,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b17"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        149.0,
        743.0,
        1106.0,
        827.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula017]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b18"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        492.0,
        813.0,
        766.0,
        853.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "so that simply adding a constant to a variable does not change its covariance with another variable.",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b20"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        150.0,
        855.0,
        1112.0,
        936.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Finally, the covariance of two sums can be written as a sum of covariances,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b21"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        207.0,
        920.0,
        1100.0,
        970.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula018]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b22"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        249.0,
        981.0,
        937.0,
        1030.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Similarly, the variance of a sum can be expressed as the sum of all possible variances and covariances. From Equations 3.10a and 3.10g,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b24"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        156.0,
        1038.0,
        1115.0,
        1121.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula019]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b25"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        415.0,
        1132.0,
        859.0,
        1176.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "More generally,",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b27"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        161.0,
        1205.0,
        355.0,
        1241.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula020]]",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b28"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        240.0,
        1254.0,
        961.0,
        1351.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Thus, the variance of a sum of uncorrelated variables is just the sum of the variances of each variable.",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b30"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        166.0,
        1360.0,
        1125.0,
        1443.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We will make considerable use of the preceding relationships in the remainder of this chapter and in chapters to come. Methods for approximating variances and covariances of more complex functions are outlined in Appendix 1.",
      "source_page": 54,
      "printed_page": 34,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p54:b31"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        167.0,
        1422.0,
        1127.0,
